WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.660
Take an ordinary copper
wire carrying 2 amps.

2
00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:06.980
Inside, free electrons
dart about at random,

3
00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:10.150
very fast, but go nowhere overall.

4
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.730
Switch on, and they pick up a
slow average drift along the wire.

5
00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:20.090
To drift the distance shown, an
electron takes about 6800 seconds:

6
00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:21.820
nearly 2 hours.

7
00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:24.240
A garden snail would win that race.

8
00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:28.970
So how can such sluggish electrons
carry a current of 2 amps?

9
00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:33.090
In this mission, you'll build an equation
for current from a simple cylinder of

10
00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:33.860
wire.

11
00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:37.490
Then you'll use it to compare
the drift in different wires.

12
00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:43.030
Your mission: explain how electrons
drifting so slowly can still carry a large

13
00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:43.730
current.
